INSERT INTO feature VALUES('effect-theories','effects','Effects as theories with laws','effect-rows','implemented','notable',NULL,'An effect is a theory of its operations, each taking what runs after it, and its laws are rules the path order orients: State''s put-get descends by branch selection, its continuation instantiated at a value of a sort no operation returns, put-put by its subterm, and two laws overlapping through a pattern join in the second-order reading. A theory lives in its module, `theory State.Eff`, and a row, a scope, a model type and a rule''s `under` row each name it by that path, declared in the source or imported. A capability scope takes a theory as its interface, `effect st : State.Eff with sort S = Integer`, the manifest fixing each value sort and the carrier left to what the scope answers, and a member it cannot read as an operation is refused by name. The scope is checked as a model of the theory: each law, read through its clauses, converts with its metavariables rigid, and a handler failing one is refused at its scope naming the law. A scope may name a module that is a model of its theory in place of the clauses, `} handle Passing`: each operation''s clause hands it to the module''s component of its name, the scope answers the module''s carrier, and it owes no law, which the module''s ascription states. A theory''s value operation, every input and its output at a sort the manifest fixes, `oper zero : N` or `oper plus : (a : N, b : N) --> N`, performs nothing: a scope naming a model takes it from the module, a law applying one is the module''s to state, and a scope writing its clauses over such a theory is refused at the value operation. A scope writing no `handle` is answered by the one model of its theory in scope at the manifest''s sorts, as a call''s omitted model is, and answers that model''s carrier as naming it does; it is refused as unanswered or ambiguous naming the theory and `} handle M`. Before it runs, a scope''s subject normalises by the theory''s laws as the path order orients them: a run of requests on the scope''s capability that instantiates a law''s left side is rewritten in place, each request its right side drops replaced by returning the value its reply stands for, its payload still checked and run once, so two reads under get-get normalise to one and the kernel replays the normal form. A returner''s row names the theory at the sorts it fixes, `-F [st : State.Eff with sort S = Integer] A`: the label''s instance is a capability over the theory''s operations at those sorts, and a scope over the theory at the same sorts discharges a call that needs it. A fixed type may name a parameter of the definition, `with sort S = a`, and each scope and exercise reads it where it stands. A fixed type naming an implicit a call omits is read at the implicit''s solution, and a scope at the sort may be what solves it.','theory-levitation kernel-term core-term core-checker surface-grammar surface-lowering','corpus/model/effects/state.gandr corpus/pathological/effects/state.gandr corpus/model/effects/state-handler.gandr corpus/pathological/effects/state-handler.gandr corpus/model/effects/state-laws.gandr corpus/pathological/effects/state-laws.gandr corpus/ledger/diagnostics/UninterfacedMember.gandr corpus/ledger/diagnostics/HandlerUnlawful.gandr crates/core-term/src/handler/subject/tests.rs crates/surface-lowering/src/lower/capability/tests.rs corpus/ledger/features/cost-effect.gandr crates/surface-lowering/tests/namespace.rs','corpus/ledger/features/effect-theories.gandr',NULL);
